WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.280 --> 00:00:04.930
LAK LAKSHMANAN:
Welcome to GCP Next.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:07.970
Today, this morning,
on the Twitter feed,

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:11.370
there was someone saying,
oh, no, here's the keynotes.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:12.530
And we have Disney.

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:13.520
We have Home Depot.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:15.200
We have HSBC.

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:16.510
Google's going all enterprise.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.294
Where's all the nerd stuff?

00:00:18.294 --> 00:00:19.460
Well, here's the nerd stuff.

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:23.466
So welcome to the
nerd stuff, right?

00:00:23.466 --> 00:00:24.810
[LAUGHS]

00:00:24.810 --> 00:00:26.610
[APPLAUSE]

00:00:26.610 --> 00:00:28.370
What we're going
to talk about today

00:00:28.370 --> 00:00:31.040
is how to data science GCP.

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.020
Who does data science is GCP?

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:34.970
Data engineers, right?

00:00:34.970 --> 00:00:37.670
So we're going to talk about
how a data engineer can

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.160
do data science
in GCP and how GCP

00:00:40.160 --> 00:00:44.390
makes that easy,
makes it auto-scaled,

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:46.410
makes it auto-awesome.

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:48.951
The popular imagination,
if I told you

00:00:48.951 --> 00:00:50.700
what machine learning
is, machine learning

00:00:50.700 --> 00:00:52.590
has been in the
air today, right?

00:00:52.590 --> 00:00:54.090
And we say what is
machine learning?

00:00:54.090 --> 00:00:56.340
People say, well, you
have lots of data.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.700
You do some really
complex math somehow

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.520
and out comes magic, right?

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.480
That's basically what
people's imagination of ML is.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.405
In reality, though, what is ML?

00:01:09.405 --> 00:01:11.910
ML is a lot of work.

00:01:11.910 --> 00:01:14.220
You're going to spend a
lot of time and effort

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.900
collecting the data.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.660
You're going to use
all your ingenuity,

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:22.470
all your knowledge,
all your experience

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.810
to organize this data.

00:01:24.810 --> 00:01:28.510
And then you're going to
basically bring your insights,

00:01:28.510 --> 00:01:32.760
your domain expertise in order
to create a model that somehow

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.840
represents what you
know about the domain

00:01:36.840 --> 00:01:40.620
that you're working in and
how it relates to the data.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.690
And then you're ready to go.

00:01:42.690 --> 00:01:44.970
Once you have this
model in place,

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:47.730
you can now train this model.

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.980
And that training is
all very automatic.

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:52.320
And then you end
up in magic, right?

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.940
You do end up in magic.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:57.000
But in order to get
there is a lot of work.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:59.370
And what GCP can
help you with is

00:01:59.370 --> 00:02:06.340
to make this work much more
tractable, much easier to do.

00:02:06.340 --> 00:02:08.729
So what we're
talking about here is

00:02:08.729 --> 00:02:13.770
that GCP is a place where data
science meets data engineering.

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:18.030
And I would like to introduce
to you my two colleagues, Reza,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.520
who's going to play the
part of a data engineer;

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:22.140
myself, I'm Lak.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.020
So we're going to play
this role of myself

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:30.000
as a CTO, the head of BI for
a company called Alphaplex.

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.370
We make widgets.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.310
And Alex here, another
of my colleagues,

00:02:35.310 --> 00:02:37.830
is going to play the
part of a data engineer.

00:02:37.830 --> 00:02:40.360
So what's the scenario?

00:02:40.360 --> 00:02:42.950
The scenario here is
that we run a factory.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.140
And in our factory
we make widgets.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:50.190
And of course a factory has a
bunch of different machines.

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:54.120
And these machines heat
up the factory floor.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:55.350
We have a factory.

00:02:55.350 --> 00:02:57.390
These machines heat
up the factory floor.

00:02:57.390 --> 00:02:59.790
We have people working
in these factories.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.275
So we have to ensure that this
factory is efficiently cooled.

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.840
We need to make sure that
we're not spending too

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.850
much on cooling the factory.

00:03:11.850 --> 00:03:16.530
But if the factory
floor gets too hot,

00:03:16.530 --> 00:03:19.300
then we are going to
result in wear and tear.

00:03:19.300 --> 00:03:21.630
So we need to figure
out the optimal way

00:03:21.630 --> 00:03:23.880
to cool the factory floor.

00:03:23.880 --> 00:03:27.610
Fortunately, we've been
collecting data, right?

00:03:27.610 --> 00:03:29.440
We've been collecting
data, and we'll

00:03:29.440 --> 00:03:32.100
be able to use
that data to solve

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:35.400
this problem of how to
efficiently cool the factory

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:36.770
floor.

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:39.380
What we need to do is
that we have a factory.

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:41.210
We have an air conditioner.

00:03:41.210 --> 00:03:45.980
And we need to figure out
when to switch on the AC.

00:03:45.980 --> 00:03:49.910
If this sounds familiar to you,
this is kind of the same issue

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:53.000
that we do in
Google data centers

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:55.520
when we need to basically figure
out how to efficiently cool

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:56.420
a data center.

00:03:56.420 --> 00:03:58.100
This is a common problem, right?

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:00.530
So we need to figure out
when to switch the AC on.

00:04:00.530 --> 00:04:02.830
We have a bunch of
different machines, right?

00:04:02.830 --> 00:04:08.420
And these machines essentially
heat up and cool down,

00:04:08.420 --> 00:04:10.100
right, the factory.

00:04:10.100 --> 00:04:12.680
So we need to basically figure
out how to keep these machines

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:15.950
and how to keep the people
at safe temperatures.

00:04:15.950 --> 00:04:19.610
And what data we have is
the inside temperature

00:04:19.610 --> 00:04:23.090
of the factory, the
outside temperature.

00:04:23.090 --> 00:04:25.640
And the reason we have
that is because we went

00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.370
to our chief scientist, Isaac.

00:04:28.370 --> 00:04:30.680
And Isaac told us
that he actually

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:32.900
has this formula, right?

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:35.750
So Isaac has this
formula that said, now,

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:37.269
if you know the
inside temperature

00:04:37.269 --> 00:04:38.810
and you have the
outside temperature,

00:04:38.810 --> 00:04:41.210
you do all of this
math, I'll tell you

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.940
that if you have a bowl of soup,
how long is it going to take

00:04:43.940 --> 00:04:46.070
that bowl of soup to cool down.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:48.789
So Isaac says he has
the exact formula.

00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:50.830
But we say, hey, Newton,
hold your horses, right?

00:04:50.830 --> 00:04:52.820
We don't want to
basically use that formula

00:04:52.820 --> 00:04:55.490
because real life is
a bit more complicated

00:04:55.490 --> 00:04:57.520
than just a bowl of soup.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:04:59.410
Why is it more complicated?

00:04:59.410 --> 00:05:02.980
Because we can monitor
the inside temperature.

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:05.590
We can monitor the
outside temperature.

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.800
But there are other
complicating factors--

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.690
complicating factors like
which machines are running,

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.580
how many machines
are running, right?

00:05:15.580 --> 00:05:18.070
What are the machines doing
and how much are the heating

00:05:18.070 --> 00:05:19.200
things up, right?

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:21.130
How many people
are in the factory.

00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:23.380
And all of these
kinds of things make

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:25.900
applying that
heuristic, that rule

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:28.510
that we had, really hard to do.

00:05:28.510 --> 00:05:31.850
And if you think about machine
learning in the real world,

00:05:31.850 --> 00:05:35.200
it's usually a
replacement for you

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:36.497
use to have a bunch of data.

00:05:36.497 --> 00:05:37.580
You used to have formulas.

00:05:37.580 --> 00:05:38.695
You used to have rules.

00:05:38.695 --> 00:05:40.870
You used to have heuristics.

00:05:40.870 --> 00:05:44.470
And now you change those
out and replace them

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:47.810
by something that
learns from the data.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:50.380
So we have collected
data over time

00:05:50.380 --> 00:05:52.780
about the factories
inside temperature,

00:05:52.780 --> 00:05:55.180
the outside
temperature, what was

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:58.850
running in the factory at
different points in time.

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:00.470
We have that data.

00:06:00.470 --> 00:06:04.180
And so we will basically
show you that journey, right?

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:07.550
But first, let's
start where we are.

00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:11.800
And let's say that we know
things about our factory floor.

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:13.360
We know the inside temperature.

00:06:13.360 --> 00:06:15.310
We know the outside temperature.

00:06:15.310 --> 00:06:19.330
Let's figure out how
to basically balance

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:22.780
the cost of cooling versus
the cost of the repairs

00:06:22.780 --> 00:06:25.570
that are going to result if
we let the temperature get

00:06:25.570 --> 00:06:26.740
too high.

00:06:26.740 --> 00:06:29.170
That's basically the balance
that we have to strike.

00:06:29.170 --> 00:06:31.245
So Reza, let's build
something cool.

00:06:31.245 --> 00:06:31.870
REZA ROKNI: OK.

00:06:31.870 --> 00:06:36.392
So I am the data engineer,
and we're a small company.

00:06:36.392 --> 00:06:38.350
And so in terms of the
number of data engineers

00:06:38.350 --> 00:06:40.300
we have, just me.

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:42.310
So we have some
constraints in terms

00:06:42.310 --> 00:06:44.710
of how much stuff
I am able to do.

00:06:44.710 --> 00:06:47.680
And one of the lines that
Lak said very casually

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:49.870
is we've been collecting data.

00:06:49.870 --> 00:06:51.790
Now, as a data
engineer, that line

00:06:51.790 --> 00:06:54.817
is potentially quite
a lot of work for me.

00:06:54.817 --> 00:06:56.650
So let's have a look
about the kind of thing

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:59.780
that we needed to put together
to actually collect that data.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:01.090
So we have this factory.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:04.090
And again, we have
one data engineer.

00:07:04.090 --> 00:07:06.730
I want to make sure that
anything that I'm writing

00:07:06.730 --> 00:07:10.017
is, first of all, production
grade, two, it will scale out.

00:07:10.017 --> 00:07:12.100
So while we might be doing
an experiment right now

00:07:12.100 --> 00:07:14.050
with one factory,
I want to make sure

00:07:14.050 --> 00:07:16.160
that if we have factories
across the globe,

00:07:16.160 --> 00:07:18.430
if we have many
thousands of IoT devices

00:07:18.430 --> 00:07:21.850
in that factory, recording
things like temperature, number

00:07:21.850 --> 00:07:24.880
of people, maybe tomorrow
it's measuring things

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:27.430
about the machines themselves
and sending that information

00:07:27.430 --> 00:07:30.100
on, no matter how
much data I have,

00:07:30.100 --> 00:07:32.890
I want to be able to always
absorb this data coming

00:07:32.890 --> 00:07:34.570
from all of these IoT devices.

00:07:34.570 --> 00:07:36.637
And it's important I
do this in stream mode.

00:07:36.637 --> 00:07:39.220
One, it's important for the data
collection and the processing

00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:42.100
but two, as we come to see
when we want to do some more

00:07:42.100 --> 00:07:44.970
inference on that data points.

00:07:44.970 --> 00:07:47.946
The next piece of collecting--
after collecting the data

00:07:47.946 --> 00:07:50.320
there's a couple of other
things I'm going to need to do.

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.840
First of all, I'm going to
need to do some processing.

00:07:52.840 --> 00:07:55.930
And again, one engineer, I
want to make sure whatever

00:07:55.930 --> 00:07:59.860
I write is very small amounts
of code and the systems

00:07:59.860 --> 00:08:01.420
that I'm using
are fully managed.

00:08:01.420 --> 00:08:03.670
So I don't have to wake up
at 3 o'clock in the morning

00:08:03.670 --> 00:08:07.000
to restart servers or to do
things like re-balance things

00:08:07.000 --> 00:08:09.040
because our loads have gone up.

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:12.200
In that processing, I'm going
to do two very simple things.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:14.860
First, I'm going to
element-wise take

00:08:14.860 --> 00:08:18.700
that data to a repository
for the propeller

00:08:18.700 --> 00:08:21.590
heads to be able to do
their smart data science.

00:08:21.590 --> 00:08:25.720
The other branch of this
is my very basic check

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.240
to when to turn
the AC system on.

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:30.640
And that is a
simple test that I'm

00:08:30.640 --> 00:08:34.299
going to run a sliding
window across the streaming

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:35.390
data that's coming in.

00:08:35.390 --> 00:08:37.996
That sliding window's going
to be five minutes long.

00:08:37.996 --> 00:08:39.370
For the purpose
of this demo, I'm

00:08:39.370 --> 00:08:41.480
just going to do it every-- the
period of that sliding window's

00:08:41.480 --> 00:08:42.776
going to be 15 seconds.

00:08:42.776 --> 00:08:44.650
So every 15 seconds that
five-minute window's

00:08:44.650 --> 00:08:45.860
going to slide down.

00:08:45.860 --> 00:08:47.890
I'm going to do a
calculation of a mean.

00:08:47.890 --> 00:08:50.140
And I'm going to do a
really simple check, which

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:53.890
is if the value is over
30-degrees centigrade,

00:08:53.890 --> 00:08:55.440
then turn the AC on.

00:08:55.440 --> 00:08:58.432
I'm from UK, so it would
be centigrade, right?

00:08:58.432 --> 00:08:59.890
So let's think
about the components

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:01.510
that I'm going to use.

00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:05.950
First of all, I want to use
Google Cloud Pub/Sub API.

00:09:05.950 --> 00:09:08.740
It's a fully managed,
published API.

00:09:08.740 --> 00:09:11.080
It meets the requirements
for it's global,

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:16.870
so I can see many factories all
sending messages to the API.

00:09:16.870 --> 00:09:18.200
I can have a single topic.

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.890
So I don't need to worry about
today I've got 100 messages

00:09:20.890 --> 00:09:21.570
a second.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:24.460
Tomorrow I have hundreds of
thousands of messages a second.

00:09:24.460 --> 00:09:25.640
I don't need to reshard.

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:27.490
I don't need to think
about partitioning.

00:09:27.490 --> 00:09:30.780
I just keep sending
information into that topic.

00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:33.850
The next piece of the puzzle
is the processing side.

00:09:33.850 --> 00:09:36.370
So I'm going to take this data
that's coming from Pub/Sub,

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:37.770
and I want to process it.

00:09:37.770 --> 00:09:41.160
One of the other advantages
that pops up is that it is HA,

00:09:41.160 --> 00:09:43.140
and it will hold onto
all information sent

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.740
to it for up to seven days until
a subscriber pulls the message

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:50.410
and also acknowledges that
that message has been removed.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:53.820
So the component that I'm going
to connect to this is Dataflow.

00:09:53.820 --> 00:09:56.070
And Dataflow allows us to
do both batch and stream

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.296
processing in our pipeline.

00:09:58.296 --> 00:10:00.420
In this case, we're just
going to use the streaming

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.620
capabilities.

00:10:01.620 --> 00:10:03.920
Dataflow will take care
of spinning up machines,

00:10:03.920 --> 00:10:06.420
alter heating, all that other
stuff that, again, I don't

00:10:06.420 --> 00:10:07.620
want to be concerned with.

00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.870
I just want to write
simple declarative code

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:11.850
on what my pipeline
should do and then

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:13.710
let the system get on with it.

00:10:13.710 --> 00:10:15.910
In that pipeline, I'm
going to have two branches.

00:10:15.910 --> 00:10:19.410
The first branch is to go to
BigQuery, which is where we're

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:20.820
going to store our information.

00:10:20.820 --> 00:10:24.490
There are multiple choices of
where you could land IoT data.

00:10:24.490 --> 00:10:26.340
Especially as it's
time series, Bigtable

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:27.540
could be another option.

00:10:27.540 --> 00:10:29.580
However, again,
[INAUDIBLE] just want

00:10:29.580 --> 00:10:32.170
to run queries
against this system,

00:10:32.170 --> 00:10:35.910
so BigQuery's a very good
place to land this data.

00:10:35.910 --> 00:10:38.880
The other branch is the
simple that I want to do.

00:10:38.880 --> 00:10:41.130
Take the mean of
the sliding window.

00:10:41.130 --> 00:10:44.220
If it's over 30 degrees,
then tell the AC

00:10:44.220 --> 00:10:46.470
to either come on or off.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.060
And in this case,
we're actually going

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:51.066
to use PubSub again, although we
haven't got it in this diagram.

00:10:51.066 --> 00:10:52.440
The reason we're
using PubSub now

00:10:52.440 --> 00:10:55.350
is not as an ingestion
mechanism, but as the glue

00:10:55.350 --> 00:10:57.310
amongst my data pipeline.

00:10:57.310 --> 00:10:59.610
So when I have a message,
I put it back into PubSub.

00:10:59.610 --> 00:11:01.560
It can communicate
with downstream systems

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.160
in the factories to be
able to turn this AC on.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:07.560
And just to see how this
would look on our pipeline

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:11.580
as it's running, could you
connect to the laptop, please?

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:12.360
Thank you.

00:11:12.360 --> 00:11:16.650
So this is the monitoring
UI from Dataflow.

00:11:16.650 --> 00:11:21.130
And it's actually showing
the pipeline in motion.

00:11:21.130 --> 00:11:25.410
We've got the IoT devices
pushing data into a topic

00:11:25.410 --> 00:11:27.140
that I am reading from.

00:11:27.140 --> 00:11:29.170
I am then parsing the IoT data.

00:11:29.170 --> 00:11:30.610
So it's coming in JSON format.

00:11:30.610 --> 00:11:32.740
I'm extracting the
bits that I need.

00:11:32.740 --> 00:11:34.290
And then it branches out.

00:11:34.290 --> 00:11:36.180
The first branch is
that element-wise,

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.670
it's sending data to BigQuery.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:40.110
And by element-wise
I mean there's

00:11:40.110 --> 00:11:41.550
no micro batching or batching.

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:43.920
So as data's flowing
in, it's immediately

00:11:43.920 --> 00:11:47.070
available to any queries
the guys want to run.

00:11:47.070 --> 00:11:50.730
On the other side, I am
doing our sliding window.

00:11:50.730 --> 00:11:53.280
So I create a five-minute
sliding window.

00:11:53.280 --> 00:11:55.380
I will then extract
the temperature

00:11:55.380 --> 00:11:58.600
from the data that exists
within that five-minute window,

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:00.720
pass it to a mean function.

00:12:00.720 --> 00:12:04.760
From there, we will
check if the value

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:06.680
is over 30-degrees centigrade.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:09.540
If it is, I'm going to
