was down because we've

00:12:46.400 --> 00:12:48.300
got these flat lines
going up, because it

00:12:48.300 --> 00:12:49.758
didn't have any
data at that point,

00:12:49.758 --> 00:12:51.200
and it's just jumped across.

00:12:51.200 --> 00:12:54.710
So this system will scale.

00:12:54.710 --> 00:12:58.320
Could we go back to
the slides, please?

00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.530
So the amount of
code I had to write--

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.900
this is the code for
the Dataflow section.

00:13:03.900 --> 00:13:06.090
For Pub/Sub, I just
created a topic.

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:09.090
There's nothing other than
a DevOps action there.

00:13:09.090 --> 00:13:10.890
In terms of the code,
these are snippets.

00:13:10.890 --> 00:13:14.490
But just to give you a flavor,
in Dataflow, data that comes in

00:13:14.490 --> 00:13:16.140
gets turned into a
parallel collection

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:17.430
of immutable objects.

00:13:17.430 --> 00:13:18.990
And what we do with
that collection

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:21.780
is we run a series of
transforms against the data.

00:13:21.780 --> 00:13:25.210
The first transform I do is
saying I want a sliding window.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:27.780
So here, those three
lines at the top--

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.670
with window into SlidingWindow,
duration 5 minutes,

00:13:32.670 --> 00:13:34.250
with a period of 15 seconds.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:36.000
Of course, if we were
doing this for real,

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:37.140
we wouldn't use 15 seconds.

00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:39.500
We don't want the AC to come
on and off that quickly.

00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:41.250
You'd put it at five
minutes, but then you

00:13:41.250 --> 00:13:44.020
wouldn't see the nice flashing
indication on the Firebase.

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:47.220
Next, I want to actually do
the calculation of the mean.

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:50.520
Mean, sum, counts, all of
these are standard things

00:13:50.520 --> 00:13:52.560
that people want to do
with data in pipelines.

00:13:52.560 --> 00:13:54.800
So there's a primitive
built into Dataflow.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:57.300
That single line of code
allows me to get the mean

00:13:57.300 --> 00:13:59.160
from every single window.

00:13:59.160 --> 00:14:02.870
Next, we do a very trivial
test, the check threshold.

00:14:02.870 --> 00:14:04.620
And that checks threshold
is nothing other

00:14:04.620 --> 00:14:07.320
than returning a Boolean
if the value of the mean

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:09.120
is over 30-degrees centigrade.

00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:11.580
So that's our very
basic pipeline.

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.844
Now we're going to hand it
over to our very smart data

00:14:13.844 --> 00:14:16.260
scientists, who are going to
do some magic with that data.

00:14:28.780 --> 00:14:31.684
ALEX OSTERLOH: So I didn't
get the mobile microphone,

00:14:31.684 --> 00:14:33.350
so I have to stand
here, which is great.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.830
Because otherwise, I run off.

00:14:35.830 --> 00:14:40.170
So from a data science point
of view, what we just saw was

00:14:40.170 --> 00:14:42.850
we have a rule in
place for deciding

00:14:42.850 --> 00:14:44.440
when to turn the AC on and off.

00:14:44.440 --> 00:14:47.260
And that rule was,
is the temperature

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:50.520
bigger than 30 or
less than 30, right?

00:14:50.520 --> 00:14:54.460
So it's like Google and
with many of our products,

00:14:54.460 --> 00:14:56.920
we've been doing this
rule-based decisions

00:14:56.920 --> 00:14:59.030
in our products a lot.

00:14:59.030 --> 00:15:00.640
And only in the
last couple of years

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.180
we turned to machine
learning to actually improve

00:15:04.180 --> 00:15:07.870
the way our products work,
make predictions, and increase

00:15:07.870 --> 00:15:08.570
the quality.

00:15:08.570 --> 00:15:13.600
So one example is if you
do a search on giants,

00:15:13.600 --> 00:15:16.391
it depends on probably
where you are.

00:15:16.391 --> 00:15:17.890
If you're in San
Francisco here, you

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:20.740
might be interested in the
San Francisco Giants probably.

00:15:20.740 --> 00:15:23.290
If you're in New
York, it might be

00:15:23.290 --> 00:15:26.380
different types of Giants,
football team versus baseball

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:27.320
team.

00:15:27.320 --> 00:15:31.240
So we used to write these
rules where you say, OK, here's

00:15:31.240 --> 00:15:33.790
the query.

00:15:33.790 --> 00:15:37.120
Then based on the location, if
it's San Francisco or New York,

00:15:37.120 --> 00:15:41.140
we give the user
different search results

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:47.522
that are focused on what the
user is probably desiring.

00:15:47.522 --> 00:15:48.730
In my case, I'm from Germany.

00:15:48.730 --> 00:15:50.770
So if I search for
giants, it might

00:15:50.770 --> 00:15:53.240
be the Dortmund Giants, which
I didn't even know existed.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:55.940
But I was searching for
a team, and, of course,

00:15:55.940 --> 00:15:59.150
there's a German Giants team.

00:15:59.150 --> 00:16:02.290
So RankBrain is a
good example of how

00:16:02.290 --> 00:16:06.970
we switch from using rules
to going to machine learning.

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:10.790
Because instead of just looking
at the search terms themselves

00:16:10.790 --> 00:16:14.830
and how often they
appear in a text

00:16:14.830 --> 00:16:17.410
and how close they
are to each other,

00:16:17.410 --> 00:16:21.160
we actually use natural
language processing to determine

00:16:21.160 --> 00:16:22.570
the relevance of a document.

00:16:22.570 --> 00:16:29.080
And this is the third
highest ranking criteria

00:16:29.080 --> 00:16:31.870
on how we determine
whether a search result is

00:16:31.870 --> 00:16:34.372
further up or further down.

00:16:34.372 --> 00:16:35.830
And this is the
biggest improvement

00:16:35.830 --> 00:16:42.540
we've seen in our search quality
in the last couple of years.

00:16:42.540 --> 00:16:46.860
So if we turn to our problem,
we want to cool a factory.

00:16:46.860 --> 00:16:51.610
We have this temperature
coming in of 27 degrees.

00:16:51.610 --> 00:16:54.540
And then we're
trying to decide how

00:16:54.540 --> 00:16:59.300
do we react if the outside
temperature's higher or lower.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:03.120
Then we have a different
number of machines running.

00:17:03.120 --> 00:17:04.829
And then you have
new factors, right?

00:17:04.829 --> 00:17:07.690
So, now, people give
off heat as well.

00:17:07.690 --> 00:17:10.089
So you might want
to consider that.

00:17:10.089 --> 00:17:12.510
But maybe there's
different factories

00:17:12.510 --> 00:17:17.040
with different factory
sizes, with different number

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:18.810
of air conditioning
units that you

00:17:18.810 --> 00:17:20.387
can set to different levels.

00:17:20.387 --> 00:17:21.970
These are all very
different criteria.

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.690
So you can see,
turning on or off

00:17:24.690 --> 00:17:28.680
the AC, based on these
rules, can become very, very

00:17:28.680 --> 00:17:32.670
hard to do or impossible to do.

00:17:32.670 --> 00:17:36.300
So machine learning has been
around for, it seems like,

00:17:36.300 --> 00:17:37.800
hundreds of years.

00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:41.040
So Isaac is thinking now,
could we use machinery

00:17:41.040 --> 00:17:43.240
for this maybe instead?

00:17:43.240 --> 00:17:49.320
So just a little 101 on machine
learning and, in this case,

00:17:49.320 --> 00:17:54.170
deep learning, which I'll show
you in a couple of minutes

00:17:54.170 --> 00:17:55.140
and how it works.

00:17:55.140 --> 00:17:57.540
Basically, it's a
supervised learning.

00:17:57.540 --> 00:17:58.720
We use deep learning here.

00:17:58.720 --> 00:18:02.070
This is one method
of machine learning

00:18:02.070 --> 00:18:05.770
that works really well for
the stuff we do in Google.

00:18:05.770 --> 00:18:11.490
And it works by taking
a lot of label data

00:18:11.490 --> 00:18:16.170
that you know this is a
cat, dog, whatever it is.

00:18:16.170 --> 00:18:19.590
You train a model
in the first step.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:23.130
Then you evaluate how
well is this doing.

00:18:23.130 --> 00:18:25.140
So you have training data set.

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:26.730
You have test data set.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:28.680
You find out the
quality, and you

00:18:28.680 --> 00:18:32.100
do this in an iterative
approach until you have

00:18:32.100 --> 00:18:34.720
the quality that you desire.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.590
And then what you can do
is you can take that model,

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:41.970
put it on a phone, build a cool
app to take a picture of a cat

00:18:41.970 --> 00:18:45.320
and decide, is this
a cat or not, right?

00:18:45.320 --> 00:18:51.120
So this would be
applying to the model.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:55.740
So we're going to also use
this for our problem here.

00:18:55.740 --> 00:18:59.460
So we are-- because
this all live,

00:18:59.460 --> 00:19:02.250
we're training a model
based on all this data

00:19:02.250 --> 00:19:05.400
that is already being
captured in BigQuery, right?

00:19:05.400 --> 00:19:08.910
So you have number of
machines, inside temperature,

00:19:08.910 --> 00:19:12.690
maybe number of people, or
other factors that you can use.

00:19:12.690 --> 00:19:14.190
And then you can
apply this model

00:19:14.190 --> 00:19:17.910
to actually make a decision
to turn on or off the AC.

00:19:20.690 --> 00:19:24.170
Whenever you do
machine learning,

00:19:24.170 --> 00:19:26.810
you're trying to optimize
for something, right?

00:19:26.810 --> 00:19:31.550
So in this case, we're
trying to minimize the cost.

00:19:31.550 --> 00:19:37.400
And cost in our case is the
cost of the wear and tear

00:19:37.400 --> 00:19:38.450
of the machines, right?

00:19:38.450 --> 00:19:40.160
Because we're saying we
don't want the temperature

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:42.260
too high because it is
bad for the machines.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:45.740
They need to run at a
certain room temperature.

00:19:45.740 --> 00:19:48.530
And we want to minimize
the cost of maintenance

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.480
of buying new machines, right?

00:19:50.480 --> 00:19:55.130
So this is just checking that
the temperature's not too high.

00:19:55.130 --> 00:19:58.820
And on the other side, we
don't want the AC running 24/7

00:19:58.820 --> 00:20:00.830
because that's going
to cost energy,

00:20:00.830 --> 00:20:03.230
and we don't want to do that.

00:20:03.230 --> 00:20:07.760
We want to be efficient in how
we use that energy to power

00:20:07.760 --> 00:20:09.140
the AC, right?

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:11.480
So we're defining a
cost factor, which

00:20:11.480 --> 00:20:13.580
is a combination of
those two factors.

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:17.360
And we're trying to
balance this out.

00:20:17.360 --> 00:20:20.720
Turns out Google has
its own factory floors,

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:22.020
right, our data centers.

00:20:22.020 --> 00:20:24.800
And we're actually applying
machine learning here

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:30.230
to optimize how we
run certain workloads.

00:20:30.230 --> 00:20:32.630
You can imagine a
lot of the work notes

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:35.580
that we do are actually around
machine learning, right?

00:20:35.580 --> 00:20:38.720
So we're updating models and
improving our recommendations

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:41.030
on cat videos and
stuff like this.

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:44.600
And it doesn't matter if this is
running in Oregon or in Finland

00:20:44.600 --> 00:20:45.540
or anywhere else.

00:20:45.540 --> 00:20:47.960
So machine learning
is a good criteria

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:50.660
to actually make
that decision for us.

00:20:50.660 --> 00:20:54.830
And we've been able to reduce
energy costs to up to 40%

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:58.580
when we apply machine learning.

00:20:58.580 --> 00:21:01.070
And there's actually a
cool white paper out.

00:21:01.070 --> 00:21:05.840
There's a URL on there where
it talks about how we do this.

00:21:05.840 --> 00:21:09.440
And these are some
of the features used

00:21:09.440 --> 00:21:15.150
to actually create a model
that is optimizing our own data

00:21:15.150 --> 00:21:15.650
centers.

