00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:43.510
So that means it's
created a model,

00:25:43.510 --> 00:25:46.750
and it's putting
it in a directory

00:25:46.750 --> 00:25:48.010
that we specified, right?

00:25:48.010 --> 00:25:50.540
So now we have a
model and a directory.

00:25:50.540 --> 00:25:53.110
And now we can do some
prediction on that data, right?

00:25:53.110 --> 00:25:56.170
So we can give it some
temperatures, some inside,

00:25:56.170 --> 00:25:58.360
outside, number of
machines running.

00:25:58.360 --> 00:26:01.910
And it's going to give us
back a cost factor, right?

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:11.950
So here comes the
important part,

00:26:11.950 --> 00:26:15.420
where we actually now determine
and evaluate how well did

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.430
our model actually do, right?

00:26:17.430 --> 00:26:19.440
So remember, we had
the training data.

00:26:19.440 --> 00:26:20.820
We have the test data.

00:26:20.820 --> 00:26:24.930
And now we want to compare the
two different data points--

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:28.530
how well did our predictions
actually do against some test

00:26:28.530 --> 00:26:32.160
data that we held back and
that wasn't used for training?

00:26:32.160 --> 00:26:35.410
So this is actually the
table we get right here.

00:26:35.410 --> 00:26:37.920
So we have the true
cost that we know about,

00:26:37.920 --> 00:26:40.980
and we have predicted cost.

00:26:40.980 --> 00:26:44.930
Since this may be little hard
to look at and understand,

00:26:44.930 --> 00:26:46.740
there's also visualization.

00:26:46.740 --> 00:26:50.190
So that's one of the cool
things, also, with Jupiter

00:26:50.190 --> 00:26:53.250
notebooks that you can use all
these visualizations libraries

00:26:53.250 --> 00:26:55.740
that are there for Python.

00:26:55.740 --> 00:26:58.260
And you get kind of
this heat map, where

00:26:58.260 --> 00:27:01.740
you, based on a certain
true cost factor,

00:27:01.740 --> 00:27:04.790
you see the spectrum of
what was predicted, right?

00:27:04.790 --> 00:27:06.960
So here you have the true cost.

00:27:06.960 --> 00:27:08.845
Here you have the
predicted cost.

00:27:08.845 --> 00:27:10.970
And this is a pretty good
visualization, giving you

00:27:10.970 --> 00:27:13.020
an idea of how
well are we doing.

00:27:13.020 --> 00:27:17.310
And maybe this is an indication
for you to go back up

00:27:17.310 --> 00:27:22.320
and maybe do some parameter
tuning and try some things out,

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:26.070
do a different split
maybe on training and test

00:27:26.070 --> 00:27:28.230
and these type of
things, until you're

00:27:28.230 --> 00:27:31.750
at a quality that is good
enough for your use case.

00:27:36.160 --> 00:27:39.970
Now I'm at the point where
we actually have a model.

00:27:39.970 --> 00:27:43.900
And what we're not doing here
is we're not training this

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:46.090
again on Cloud ML
on a billion data

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:49.060
points because we don't
have the time for you all

00:27:49.060 --> 00:27:52.060
to see how long this takes.

00:27:52.060 --> 00:27:54.550
So we are going to keep
the model as we have it.

00:27:54.550 --> 00:27:59.650
And we're going to push
it in Cloud ML, which

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:03.340
means I can give
Reza, in a second,

00:28:03.340 --> 00:28:09.460
an API call that he can use
to ping the Cloud ML API

00:28:09.460 --> 00:28:11.206
and give it some values.

00:28:11.206 --> 00:28:12.580
And these are the
values that are

00:28:12.580 --> 00:28:14.830
going to come in though
PubSub and Dataflow,

00:28:14.830 --> 00:28:22.750
give him some values and
make a prediction on what

00:28:22.750 --> 00:28:25.390
action should be taken.

00:28:25.390 --> 00:28:28.570
So what you could do, actually,
is you give it two numbers.

00:28:28.570 --> 00:28:32.140
You give it maybe the current
temperature indications.

00:28:32.140 --> 00:28:37.810
And then you can say, OK,
the way it's going right now,

00:28:37.810 --> 00:28:39.700
the way the temperature's
moving right now,

00:28:39.700 --> 00:28:42.010
what if the temperature's
one degree higher or two

00:28:42.010 --> 00:28:45.730
degrees higher, what
would my actual cost be?

00:28:45.730 --> 00:28:47.320
So you get two values back.

00:28:47.320 --> 00:28:49.390
And that could be an
indication for you

00:28:49.390 --> 00:28:52.420
to take action now
or wait two minutes

00:28:52.420 --> 00:28:54.850
because the cost,
actually, of turning

00:28:54.850 --> 00:28:58.570
the AC on in two minutes is
going to be actually lower.

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:02.560
So this is the type
of back and forth

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:07.660
that I can now give to Reza
that he can now, hopefully,

00:29:07.660 --> 00:29:10.720
without having to tear down
his pipeline that he built,

00:29:10.720 --> 00:29:14.050
can actually use
in the architecture

00:29:14.050 --> 00:29:18.490
that he'll be
demonstrating in a second.

00:29:18.490 --> 00:29:19.140
Great.

00:29:19.140 --> 00:29:22.130
So I think we go back
to slides, right?

00:29:22.130 --> 00:29:25.810
REZA ROKNI: Yeah, thanks.

00:29:25.810 --> 00:29:29.200
So the key component
that I'm actually

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:32.770
going to have to swap out
is that check that I was

00:29:32.770 --> 00:29:35.190
doing against the temperature.

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:36.940
So before, if we
remember our pipeline,

00:29:36.940 --> 00:29:40.960
we had IoT devices all sending
streams of data to PubSub.

00:29:40.960 --> 00:29:43.757
PubSub was being
consumed by Dataflow.

00:29:43.757 --> 00:29:45.340
We were splitting
the data, sending it

00:29:45.340 --> 00:29:48.640
to BigQuery, which allowed
Alex to be able to do his work.

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:51.280
On the other side, we
had that simple test,

00:29:51.280 --> 00:29:53.500
where I was doing a
sliding window of five

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:56.290
minutes, every 15 seconds,
calculating the mean,

00:29:56.290 --> 00:29:57.400
and doing a check.

00:29:57.400 --> 00:30:00.190
Now I just need to replace
that with the inference

00:30:00.190 --> 00:30:01.240
that we have now.

00:30:01.240 --> 00:30:04.430
And I also no longer
need that sliding window.

00:30:04.430 --> 00:30:07.330
So that sliding window was just
there for me to get an average,

00:30:07.330 --> 00:30:08.970
rather than go point by point.

00:30:08.970 --> 00:30:13.510
Here, the system can just
take each point by the values

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:16.552
that we have and then
give us a prediction back.

00:30:16.552 --> 00:30:18.010
So the one thing
I'll need to do is

00:30:18.010 --> 00:30:21.370
change from that check
threshold, where I was just

00:30:21.370 --> 00:30:24.160
checking the temp value
against 30 degrees,

00:30:24.160 --> 00:30:27.450
to the new code, which is
to call the Inference API.

00:30:27.450 --> 00:30:32.620
And the Inference
endpoint is the one

00:30:32.620 --> 00:30:35.260
that has been deployed
with that version number.

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:38.470
We have the package shape
that I need to send to it.

00:30:38.470 --> 00:30:40.400
So that packet shape
is a JSON structure

00:30:40.400 --> 00:30:41.650
I'll need to put together.

00:30:41.650 --> 00:30:44.500
That JSON structure
comes from the same data

00:30:44.500 --> 00:30:46.310
that I was collecting before.

00:30:46.310 --> 00:30:49.390
So in order to actually
switch this out,

00:30:49.390 --> 00:30:53.020
I will just need to replace
my checksum with a REST call

00:30:53.020 --> 00:30:54.942
to the Cloud ML service.

00:30:54.942 --> 00:30:56.650
And then from there,
the Cloud ML service

00:30:56.650 --> 00:30:58.840
is going to give me
a prediction back.

00:30:58.840 --> 00:31:00.970
That prediction back
will be in JSON format.

00:31:00.970 --> 00:31:03.850
I need to just extract
the piece of data

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:06.410
that says whether the fan
should come on or off.

00:31:06.410 --> 00:31:08.700
And then through the same
part as the pipeline,

00:31:08.700 --> 00:31:12.500
I will actually send
it on to the factory.

00:31:12.500 --> 00:31:14.850
And it will decide whether
to issue the on or off.

00:31:14.850 --> 00:31:17.150
So if we could just go back
to this laptop, please.

00:31:21.280 --> 00:31:24.430
So previously, the section
that we hadn't shown

00:31:24.430 --> 00:31:29.820
was this middle bit, which
was taking that data.

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:32.340
And instead of doing the
five-minutes sliding window,

00:31:32.340 --> 00:31:34.710
it's actually calling
the protection service.

00:31:34.710 --> 00:31:36.690
We get the output from
the prediction service.

00:31:36.690 --> 00:31:39.150
I have a simple
JSON deserialization

00:31:39.150 --> 00:31:41.610
to take the values that
I want out from that.

00:31:41.610 --> 00:31:45.750
And then I send it again on
to both PubSub and Firebase.

00:31:45.750 --> 00:31:49.860
If we go to Firebase, I'll
actually go up a level now.

00:31:49.860 --> 00:31:51.900
We can see two data points.

00:31:51.900 --> 00:31:54.750
One was the original
flow, which has got check

00:31:54.750 --> 00:31:56.100
whether over 30 degrees.

00:31:56.100 --> 00:31:58.560
The other one is
now the inference

00:31:58.560 --> 00:32:01.410
being called from
the Cloud ML service.

00:32:01.410 --> 00:32:03.000
And the reason,
actually, we'd want

00:32:03.000 --> 00:32:06.810
to keep both running is,
like any good data engineer,

00:32:06.810 --> 00:32:09.330
I'm not going to just
switch over from production

00:32:09.330 --> 00:32:10.560
in a second, right?

00:32:10.560 --> 00:32:11.550
This is all good stuff.

00:32:11.550 --> 00:32:13.134
I trust Alex has
done a great job.

00:32:13.134 --> 00:32:15.300
But I want to make sure
everything continues working

00:32:15.300 --> 00:32:17.109
before I make that switch.

00:32:17.109 --> 00:32:19.400
And this is where we start
doing the AB testing, right?

00:32:19.400 --> 00:32:23.400
In this case, we're AB testing
a very old, basic method

00:32:23.400 --> 00:32:26.670
of checking when the fan should
come on with the new inference

00:32:26.670 --> 00:32:27.600
model.

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:29.520
But as time moves
on, let's say this

00:32:29.520 --> 00:32:32.880
is all very good, successful,
and I actually make the switch,

00:32:32.880 --> 00:32:35.130
we're going to have
different models being built.

00:32:35.130 --> 00:32:38.250
So maybe Alex decides that
actually given the data set,

00:32:38.250 --> 00:32:40.330
every week we want
to rebuild the model.

00:32:40.330 --> 00:32:41.880
Maybe it's every day.

00:32:41.880 --> 00:32:44.437
Whatever it is, the
Cloud ML service

00:32:44.437 --> 00:32:46.770
will allow you to create
different versions of the model

00:32:46.770 --> 00:32:47.520
as you do that.

00:32:47.520 --> 00:32:49.710
So you V1, V2, V3, V4.

00:32:49.710 --> 00:32:51.630
This allows me to
continue to make sure

00:32:51.630 --> 00:32:53.970
that I can do AB testing
of that new model.

00:32:53.970 --> 00:32:55.500
So if it's happening
over the day

00:32:55.500 --> 00:32:57.150
and there's a new
model landing, I

00:32:57.150 --> 00:33:00.260
can maybe decide that what I'll
always do as a matter of course

00:33:00.260 --> 00:33:03.510
is 30% of the workload
will go into the new model.

00:33:03.510 --> 00:33:05.520
70% will continue the old model.

00:33:05.520 --> 00:33:08.860
Once I'm happy everything's in
place, I can make the switch.

00:33:08.860 --> 00:33:11.490
Dataflow allows you
to do in-situ updates.

00:33:11.490 --> 00:33:14.880
So you can actually update
the data flow as it's running.

00:33:14.880 --> 00:33:17.570
It will take care of
redistributing the code.

00:33:17.570 --> 00:33:20.340
And at that point,
switch onto the new model

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:22.969
until another model comes
along and I switch it back out.

00:33:22.969 --> 00:33:24.510
And these are the
kind of things that

00:33:24.510 --> 00:33:27.510
allows us to take
the data science

00:33:27.510 --> 00:33:30.520
and actually make in
production and usable very,

00:33:30.520 --> 00:33:31.960
very efficiently.

00:33:31.960 --> 00:33:33.362
So back over to Lak.

00:33:33.362 --> 00:33:34.320
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Thanks.

00:33:34.320 --> 00:33:35.920
Could we get back
to the demo machine?

00:33:42.972 --> 00:33:44.430
REZA ROKNI: Do you
want the slides?

00:33:44.430 --> 00:33:45.470
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Yeah,
I want the demo.

00:33:45.470 --> 00:33:46.260
REZA ROKNI: The demo machine?

00:33:46.260 --> 00:33:48.176
LAK LAKSHMANAN: Yes, the
demo machine, please.

00:33:48.176 --> 00:33:53.619
Right, so just to recap,
the first thing is, in order

00:33:53.619 --> 00:33:56.160
to do something like this, in
order to do something like this

00:33:56.160 --> 00:34:00.690
to make it easy, notice that
the first thing that Reza did

00:34:00.690 --> 00:34:03.690
was that he made sure
that he took all the data.

00:34:03.690 --> 00:34:05.850
Before he did all
the sliding windows,

00:34:05.850 --> 00:34:08.280
before he did all
the aggregations,

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:12.630
there is this nice little thing
that says Write Raw Data to BQ.

00:34:12.630 --> 00:34:14.120
That's where everything starts.

00:34:14.120 --> 00:34:17.000
If you're not storing data,
you're not going anywhere.

00:34:17.000 --> 00:34:19.090
It's not going to anyplace
that's auto-awesome.

00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:21.540
The first thing
is save your data.

00:34:21.540 --> 00:34:24.750
And once you have saved
the data, we can then go,

00:34:24.750 --> 00:34:27.989
and that's basically, that
is the data that Alex could

00:34:27.989 --> 00:34:30.300
basically use in his Datalab.

00:34:30.300 --> 00:34:33.850
And you'll notice that his
Datalab started with BigQuery.

00:34:33.850 --> 00:34:37.650
He went into BigQuery query and
said, let me go ahead and pull

00:34:37.650 --> 00:34:40.260
my data, right-- bq.Query right?

00:34:40.260 --> 00:34:43.888
That's essentially
the basic first step

00:34:43.888 --> 00:34:45.179
that you need to be able to do.

00:34:45.179 --> 00:34:48.449
You need to be saving
the data in order

00:34:48.449 --> 00:34:52.469
to go back and replace
the kinds of systems

00:34:52.469 --> 00:34:55.420
that you are doing now
with something better.

00:34:55.420 --> 00:34:58.110
I cannot emphasize this more.

00:34:58.110 --> 00:34:59.830
Make sure to save your data.

00:34:59.830 --> 00:35:02.310
Notice some of the other
things that happened, right?

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:07.710
As Alex built the Datalab
model and he deployed it,

00:35:07.710 --> 00:35:11.070
it was a very simple
Cloud ML deploy.

00:35:11.070 --> 00:35:13.230
It went onto the cloud.

00:35:13.230 --> 00:35:15.750
And he essentially got
something that could

00:35:15.750 --> 00:35:17.970
be called with a REST API.

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:19.770
And Reza knew that
he didn't have

00:35:19.770 --> 00:35:22.890
to worry that this model
that Alex had built--

00:35:22.890 --> 00:35:24.420
built by a scientist--

00:35:24.420 --> 00:35:26.130
was actually going to
work in production.

00:35:26.130 --> 00:35:29.100
It was because it's basically
running in the Cloud ML

00:35:29.100 --> 00:35:30.060
service.

00:35:30.060 --> 00:35:35.460
And Reza could simply
invoke a REST API call

00:35:35.460 --> 00:35:37.170
