00:36:08.552 --> 00:36:10.218
Could we get back to
the slides, please?

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:21.680
So bottom line, then, is that
this is now an amazing time

00:36:21.680 --> 00:36:24.542
to be a data engineer
and a data scientist.

00:36:24.542 --> 00:36:26.000
And the reason it's
an amazing time

00:36:26.000 --> 00:36:28.130
to be a data engineer
or a data scientist

00:36:28.130 --> 00:36:30.770
is that the amount of
work that you need to do

00:36:30.770 --> 00:36:33.500
has gone dramatically down.

00:36:33.500 --> 00:36:35.240
If you're thinking
about building machine

00:36:35.240 --> 00:36:37.160
learning models,
the amount of work

00:36:37.160 --> 00:36:39.830
that you need to do to build
a machine learning model

00:36:39.830 --> 00:36:42.860
fits in a single
Datalab notebook.

00:36:42.860 --> 00:36:45.080
And if you looked at the
code for that single Datalab

00:36:45.080 --> 00:36:49.670
notebook, as a first start, it
was all like structured data,

00:36:49.670 --> 00:36:51.620
go ahead and preprocess,
structured data,

00:36:51.620 --> 00:36:52.810
go ahead and train.

00:36:52.810 --> 00:36:56.600
It was single calls
on the Python API,

00:36:56.600 --> 00:37:01.250
a very easy way to get started
doing machine learning,

00:37:01.250 --> 00:37:03.050
starting with structured data.

00:37:03.050 --> 00:37:08.780
And a great tend to be a data
engine engineer because, again,

00:37:08.780 --> 00:37:12.020
you can write Dataflow code
and have all the DevOps

00:37:12.020 --> 00:37:14.480
and have all the auto-scaling
and all of these things

00:37:14.480 --> 00:37:17.630
be taken care of by
the cloud, right?

00:37:17.630 --> 00:37:20.450
So what exactly
does auto-awesome

00:37:20.450 --> 00:37:25.670
mean to you as a data engineer
or as a data scientist?

00:37:25.670 --> 00:37:27.890
As a data engineer,
I think what it

00:37:27.890 --> 00:37:30.960
means is that whether
you're doing ingestion--

00:37:30.960 --> 00:37:33.890
notice that Reza started
out ingesting data

00:37:33.890 --> 00:37:36.000
with cloud SubPub.

00:37:36.000 --> 00:37:39.300
And it could scale
from tens of messages

00:37:39.300 --> 00:37:43.740
to millions of messages,
from local to global.

00:37:43.740 --> 00:37:45.540
And he didn't have to
lift a finger, right?

00:37:45.540 --> 00:37:47.910
He just set up a
topic, and that was it.

00:37:47.910 --> 00:37:51.180
And then anybody could
basically post into that topic.

00:37:51.180 --> 00:37:53.800
The ingestion was auto-awesome.

00:37:53.800 --> 00:37:57.030
Transformation, how did
Reza do the transformation?

00:37:57.030 --> 00:38:00.170
He used Apache Beam
Cloud Dataflow.

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:02.110
He wrote his code
in Apache Beam,

00:38:02.110 --> 00:38:03.960
which is totally open source.

00:38:03.960 --> 00:38:07.590
He executed it using
Cloud Dataflow on GCP.

00:38:07.590 --> 00:38:12.810
And that essentially, again,
enabled the transformation code

00:38:12.810 --> 00:38:15.390
to be automatic, right?

00:38:15.390 --> 00:38:19.620
And we talked about how you can
basically deploy a new service,

00:38:19.620 --> 00:38:22.560
and you can replace
a running pipeline.

00:38:22.560 --> 00:38:24.300
How amazing is
that, right, to be

00:38:24.300 --> 00:38:26.150
able to replace a
running pipeline,

00:38:26.150 --> 00:38:30.300
to not lose any messages
in the process of replacing

00:38:30.300 --> 00:38:31.200
a running pipeline?

00:38:31.200 --> 00:38:32.414
That's the key thing, right?

00:38:32.414 --> 00:38:34.830
When we say you can replace a
running pipeline, what we're

00:38:34.830 --> 00:38:37.860
